※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4395
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4395 LANG_CONSTINIT_HAS_AN_EFFECT

※ **The initializer of a `constinit` variable is pure.**  It runs before the
※ program does, at a moment the program does not choose, and only where
※ something kept reads the variable -- one nothing reads is not run at all --
※ so what it does cannot be anything a reader would see happen.  It works out
※ a value, reads memory and calls pure functions; a call of an `@[impure]`
※ function, a system call, a write of a variable at the top level are each
※ refused, where they are written.  What has to be done for its effect is a
※ constructor's work.
※
※ Compare: **C++**'s `constinit`, which allows only a constant initializer and
※ so nothing with an effect, where its dynamic initialization allows anything,
※ in an order nobody chooses; **Zig**, whose `comptime` values are pure because
※ there is nothing at compile time to have an effect on; **Rust**'s
※ `LazyLock`, which runs anything on first read.

let std := ⎕import("std")

let counted: mut u64 = 0u64

@[impure]
fn count() → u64:
    counted ← counted + 1u64
    counted

constinit let first: u64 = count()

constinit let pid: i64 = ⎕syscall(⎕sc@getpid)

@[startup]
fn main(init: std.Init) → u6:
    ⎕narrow(first + ⎕narrow(pid, ⌜u64⌝) ?? 0u64, ⌜u6⌝) ?? 0u6
